1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
 - So it's always fun to talk about security

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:04,260
 because the question is, is you,

3
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:05,840
 and what does security mean to you,

4
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,680
 and what does it mean to me?

5
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:11,860
 And whenever you're thinking about security,

6
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:14,680
 you gotta figure out sort of who is out to get you

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,320
 and why are they out to get you.

8
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:22,880
 And for us average people, we are so boring

9
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:24,520
 that literally pretty much the only thing

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:27,800
 the world wants from us is they wanna steal our,

11
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:30,360
 you know, they wanna steal our credit card numbers

12
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:33,260
 and then buy stuff with them for just a couple of days

13
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:35,760
 before the credit card company shuts them down.

14
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,520
 Now, if you're running for public office,

15
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:40,080
 there's a whole bunch of your opponents

16
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:42,480
 that are gonna want to break into your email

17
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,320
 and that happens, right?

18
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,720
 Those kind of shenanigans happen.

19
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:47,440
 So if you're interesting or influential

20
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:48,960
 or running for public office,

21
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,000
 you have a different problem

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,920
 than if you're just kind of a mere civilian like us.

23
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,920
 And so at the end of the day, you can worry about security

24
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,760
 and you can kind of wear tin foil clothing or something,

25
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,000
 but at the end of the day,

26
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,480
 you and I are so boring that nobody really cares.

27
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:10,480
 But it's good to assume that they're out to get you, right?

28
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,800
 That you try to be as secure as you can

29
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:14,320
 and be aware of your environment

30
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,680
 and be as secure as you possibly can

31
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:20,460
 with your information and with your communications.

32
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,960
 So who is out to get us?

33
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,760
 Well, the government.

34
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,400
 So now we're gonna actually turn the tables, right?

35
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:32,640
 In the very first lecture, we were the good guys.

36
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,240
 The good guys were the ones

37
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,300
 who were trying to break the Kurtag free.

38
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,400
 And the bad guys were the ones

39
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,100
 that were trying to hide their bad messages.

40
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:43,180
 And the government were the good guys in Bletchley Park

41
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,900
 and they broke the codes

42
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:47,300
 and they saved Western civilization.

43
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:48,840
 Yay, good guys.

44
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:53,240
 Well now, we're the Germans.

45
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:54,760
 We're the Japanese.

46
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:56,600
 We're trying to communicate.

47
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:57,640
 People are watching.

48
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,640
 And guess what?

49
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,040
 It's the same people.

50
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,280
 The government, the government's most likely interested

51
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:05,080
 in what you're doing.

52
00:02:05,080 --> 00:02:06,880
 Again, most of us are so boring,

53
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:08,980
 the government doesn't really care at all.

54
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:12,300
 But the government has probably the best security

55
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:14,380
 breaking equipment on the planet right now,

56
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:15,800
 better than the criminals.

57
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:19,000
 And so it's just an interesting flip of the model, right?

58
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,740
 Where now we are trying to communicate

59
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,760
 and the government with all of their bombas

60
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:29,480
 and little rotating devices sitting in some warehouse

61
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,960
 somewhere that we don't know

62
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:33,320
 is taking our private communications

63
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,480
 and they're trying to break them.

64
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:40,240
 And we are in a war trying to make our communications

65
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:42,560
 more and more secure while the government says,

66
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:46,020
 whoa, we have to invent like electronic computers.

67
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:48,820
 Because we have to have bigger computers

68
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:51,160
 because the people, us good guys now

69
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,600
 who are doing the encrypting,

70
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:53,840
 we are trying to come up with more

71
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:56,000
 and more clever ways to encrypt.

72
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,120
 And the government has to keep coming up with ways to decrypt.

73
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:04,280
 And you could cast this as criminals versus good folks.

74
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:06,960
 You could cast these governments versus governments.

75
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,600
 At the end of the day, it's an arms race

76
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:12,960
 and it's rather symmetric as to which side you're on, right?

77
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:14,940
 I mean, we started out with the people

78
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,320
 trying to hide the information, we're the bad guys.

79
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,040
 And now we're the good guys.

80
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:19,960
 Why are we the good guys?

81
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:21,000
 'Cause it's us.

82
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,720
 As soon as we decide it's us, we're the good guys.

83
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,960
 Here's a little picture I took a couple of years ago

84
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:31,120
 when I visited Bletchley Park.

85
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:34,440
 This actually led to the video a year and a half later.

86
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,320
 And in this, my friend who works at the Open University,

87
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:43,520
 who also does massively open online courses,

88
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:44,840
 that's Joel, he's now retired.

89
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:49,000
 He's the tour guide there and a dear and personal friend.

90
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,720
 And another of my dear and personal friends

91
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:54,480
 whose face you can't see is right here.

92
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:55,920
 His name is Chuck.

93
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,080
 He currently works at Shazam in London.

94
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,000
 And he and I just got away for the weekend

95
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,440
 and went up to Bletchley Park and took a tour.

96
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,640
 Now, just a little interesting connection.

97
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:09,760
 I sort of mentioned in a previous lecture about The Matrix.

98
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:13,380
 So you notice that Chuck here is wearing a jacket

99
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:14,520
 from the movie Matrix.

100
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,560
 I mentioned he was the one that wrote

101
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:19,840
 the hacking scene with Trinity.

102
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:21,040
 So here we all are together.

103
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:22,400
 We're at Bletchley Park.

104
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:24,280
 Alan Turing had an office in this.

105
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,240
 This is one of Alan Turing's offices at Bletchley Park.

106
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,040
 Here's Joel, open teaching and learning.

107
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,560
 Here is Chuck, worked on The Matrix.

108
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:35,520
 And here's Trinity, right?

109
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:38,640
 Except that now Trinity is our enemy instead of our friend.

110
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,840
 Although in the movie, we are rooting for Trinity.

111
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:43,520
 But we are the ones trying to protect

112
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:46,520
 what we're doing from the Trinities of the world.

113
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,680
 We're the good guys now and Trinity is the bad gal.

114
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,760
 She's the bad lady trying to break into our stuff.

115
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:54,920
 And we're protecting ourselves against Trinity.

116
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:59,300
 So tables have completely turned, OK?

117
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:04,560
 So before we go too far, you'll run into your organizations

118
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:07,240
 with lots of people who are like security experts.

119
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:09,520
 And I find security experts generally

120
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:17,720
 very annoying because they think they're smarter than they are.

121
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,160
 Security experts have this theory

122
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:21,440
 that more security is better.

123
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,400
 And the reality is that perfect security is unachievable.

124
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:30,160
 If you want perfect security, like lock yourself in a room

125
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,320
 and whisper to somebody, oh, wait a sec.

126
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:34,800
 And then the guard will come in and hear you or whatever.

127
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:36,960
 There'll be a microphone.

128
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:39,800
 So the notion that perfect security and that your company

129
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,560
 can spend more and more and more money of its budget

130
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,960
 on security just because the security expert thinks

131
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:48,480
 that you should is really kind of a fallacy.

132
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,800
 Security is a cost benefit analysis.

133
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,800
 And so if you think of credit card companies,

134
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:56,440
 what's the security of this number?

135
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:57,680
 I won't show you the number.

136
00:05:57,680 --> 00:05:59,320
 But what if I lose this number?

137
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:00,800
 What's the consequences?

138
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,720
 I've had many credit cards that got compromised.

139
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:05,400
 I don't exactly know how.

140
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:07,640
 They've got software inside of the computer companies

141
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:10,920
 that catches, like when all of a sudden I live in Michigan

142
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:13,560
 and a bunch of charges at the New Jersey Walmart are showing up

143
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,400
 and I never got on a plane.

144
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,480
 I'm amazed at how rare it is that your identity actually

145
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:21,040
 gets lost permanently.

146
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:24,800
 So I'm really impressed with how the banks are capable.

147
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:26,160
 And so they have this number that

148
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:27,920
 makes it real convenient for us to buy.

149
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:29,480
 We can type it into a web form.

150
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:31,120
 We can do all these things.

151
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:34,200
 And one in a zillion times it gets compromised.

152
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:36,760
 And so they lose $50.

153
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,200
 But billions of dollars of commerce happen.

154
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,960
 So there is always a security versus cost.

155
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:44,400
 If basically, for me to use this credit card,

156
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:49,680
 I had to have sort of a personal guard from the company

157
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:52,680
 with me all the time, it'd be very expensive for me

158
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:55,400
 to buy stuff on Amazon and have it shipped to my house.

159
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:59,880
 And so understand that when you start getting into conversations

160
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:05,440
 with security, professionals, sometimes they're

161
00:07:05,440 --> 00:07:08,040
 the kind that understand that security is naturally

162
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:10,880
 imperfect and everything is a cost-benefit analysis,

163
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:13,240
 rather than more security is always better.

164
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:15,200
 So more is not always better.

165
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,440
 That's just my little speech about security

166
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:20,640
 in case you get stuck in a conversation with some pompous

167
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:22,200
 security expert.

168
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:23,840
 They're mostly pompous.

169
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:26,840
 So database administrator pompous, too.

170
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:30,680
 But hope you're not a database administrator security expert.

171
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:34,960
 But the best security experts that I've ever met

172
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,200
 are the ones that understand this.

173
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:40,280
 And they can characterize every issue

174
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:42,600
 in terms of a cost-benefit analysis.

175
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:44,480
 And then you know that you've run

176
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:46,700
 into one that's really smart and bright.

177
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:48,500
 So the terminology that we're going

178
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,760
 to use for the rest of the segments of this lecture,

179
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:55,280
 we're going to be solving two basic problems.

180
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:58,200
 One is confidentiality.

181
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:03,140
 And the confidentiality is the leakage of information.

182
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:09,160
 So if I was to want to send this credit card number to one

183
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:11,480
 of you watching right now without the rest of you

184
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:15,520
 seeing my credit card number, the mere revealing

185
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:18,120
 of that information is the problem.

186
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:21,480
 So I want to come up with a way to send it to you

187
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,600
 so that no one except the one person that I want to see it

188
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:25,440
 gets it.

189
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:27,600
 That's confidentiality.

190
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,520
 The other problem is one of integrity.

191
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:35,400
 And that is, in a sense, knowing that A,

192
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:37,240
 that the information that you've got

193
00:08:37,240 --> 00:08:40,400
 comes from who you thought it came from,

194
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:44,480
 and B, that it wasn't modified on the way.

195
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:46,760
 So there are things like digital signatures

196
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:49,040
 that sort of fall into this category

197
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:51,480
 and other mechanisms that we'll talk about.

198
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:55,800
 So these two themes of confidentiality and integrity

199
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:58,320
 will run through the rest of the lectures

200
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:00,480
 that we have on security.

201
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:05,080
 So coming up next, we're going to start diving right in.

